{
  "analysis": "The review describes a wheel-spin bonus in Cash Frenzy. The reviewer's complaint is that after the wheel visually stops on a large prize, the genie character moves the wheel to a different (lower) segment, so the player ends up with a much smaller payout (100 million with a 5x multiplier falling to 48 million). This is described as a deliberate, recurring design behavior rather than a bug, and the request for 'a player choice to keep or spin' is a suggestion for a non-existent feature, which is not codable under R12. The active mechanic being described is the game overriding the result the player saw: the wheel appears to land on a great prize and is then moved, which is a claim that the game manipulates the determination of an outcome and deceives the player about what they actually won. No real-money stake, purchase, or wager is mentioned, so Gambling is not triggered; the span is an outcome-manipulation claim, which matches Illusion of Control.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price, from 100 million with 5x multiplier to a 48 million",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def. darkpattern.games: 'The game cheats or hides information to make you think you're better than you actually are.'; boundary rule: 'Assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes... engineered near-misses.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the wheel visibly stops on a great prize and the game then moves it to a lower-prize segment, i.e., the game manipulates the outcome after it is revealed, deceiving the player about what they won."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes three distinct experiences. First, a 30-second wait to connect to a match, which is matchmaking/queue time rather than a designed game gate, and the codebook explicitly excludes queue and matchmaking waits from Playing by Appointment. Second, rewards that 'seem' generous but are 'just for show,' which is a complaint about the value of rewards, not about how rewards are delivered, so Reward Mania does not apply. Third, difficulty obtaining weapon upgrades or perks, which leaves the player 'stuck battling to win' — this names the repeated activity (battling) that the player must perform to make progress. The closing line is a general evaluation that the game wastes the player's time, which by itself is not a mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress' and boundary rule requiring the span to identify the repeated activity required for progress",
      "rationale": "The span says upgrades/perks are hard to obtain and implies the player must keep battling repeatedly to earn them, describing the repetitive task cycle required to advance."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes ranked PvP in Clash Royale. The reviewer first complains that at high trophy counts (8k+) every opponent uses the same three cards (Mega Knight, PEKKA, Golem), which they find annoying to defend — this is a description of competing against other players in the game's ladder. The second and dominant complaint is that the game is pay to win: reaching higher ranks cannot be done through skill alone, and each update makes the pay-to-win nature worse and buffs cards players dislike. There is no mention of timers, currencies, ads, social invites, or collection mechanics. The two mechanics described are PvP competition and a paid advantage for progress/winning.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator (darkpattern.games: 'A player can spend real money to purchase something that gives them an advantage in the game.') and Pay Wall indicator (further progress contingent on payment)",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that advancing to higher trophies is blocked without paying, i.e., spending money grants an in-game advantage that skill alone cannot match, and the game becomes harder to progress in without payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition / indicator: 'The game makes you compete against other players.'",
      "rationale": "The span describes the player being matched against other players in a trophy-based ladder, which is direct player-versus-player competition structured by the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review contains three distinct sections. The first is a customer-service complaint: a game element failed to appear within the allowed moves, the studio would not compensate 9 diamonds, and the reviewer is warning others about that experience. This is a technical fault/bug report, not a deliberate design rule, so it cannot support a dark-pattern label. The middle part, 'you don't have to pay to win,' explicitly denies that payment is required for progress, which is the opposite of describing a Pay to Progress mechanic. The last section, 'I suggest after this cust. svc experience that you don't buy from this game beyond the pass. Get friends and a team and trade lives,' names the paid Season Pass as the purchase boundary and then describes the in-game social resource exchange of lives with friends and team members already playing. I searched for what 'the pass' refers to in Fishdom because the review does not describe its mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Indicators: 'Battle passes, MMO-subscriptions'; R5: a span that names an instance assigns its meso label; worked example where naming a monthly pass ('welkin') assigned Recurring Fee.",
      "rationale": "The search established that 'the pass' in Fishdom is a monthly Season Pass purchasable for real money with exclusive rewards, a battle-pass instance of Recurring Fee; the reviewer names it as the thing beyond which they advise not buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Reciprocity definition: 'Patterns that instill a sense of obligation to reciprocate by donating resources to other players'; boundary rule: exchanging resources or lives with people ALREADY playing codes Reciprocity, not Forced Fellowship.",
      "rationale": "'trade lives' names a resource-transfer mechanism between friends and team members already in the game, which is the Reciprocity pattern regardless of the suggestion's tone."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "what is the pass in Fishdom game",
  "search_result": "The Fishdom game offers a monthly Season Pass that provides access to exclusive rewards and super challenges; the pass is optional and can be purchased for real money and ends when the event concludes."
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is mostly positive and offers suggestions. Reading the suggestions reveals the underlying mechanics. 'maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life' describes an existing life-energy regeneration timer, where a new life only becomes available after a wait; the reviewer is asking to shorten the current wait, so the wait itself is the active mechanic. 'just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes' describes a daily prize calendar that resets the player's progress when a day is missed, i.e., a daily-login incentive with a penalty for absence. 'not as many small and unnecessary tasks like celebrating' is a complaint about excess animations/tasks but describes no mechanism that blocks progress or prevents stopping, so it does not satisfy any label. Nothing here involves spending money, ads, grinding, or other patterns.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play (Hadan et al. 2024): 'Patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.'",
      "rationale": "The suggestion presupposes an existing timer that gates a new life behind a wait; the reviewer is asking to shorten that wait, so the arbitrary life-regeneration schedule is the active mechanic (R12 codes the existing behavior the suggestion refers to)."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Motivates players to log in daily by providing incentives, while also penalising them for failing to do so.'",
      "rationale": "The daily prize calendar penalises the player for missing a day by resetting the reward progress, which is the active consequence the reviewer objects to."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing an in-game ad interstitial that traps them: the ad appears, has no close button, any tap redirects to the store, returning from the store leaves the game stuck on the ad's last frame, and the only way out is killing the app. Two design behaviours are being described. First, an advertisement the player is compelled to view because it cannot be dismissed through any intended control. Second, an interface that removes the opt-out entirely (no close mark) and routes every interaction toward the store, which is an obstruction of exit combined with direction toward a purchase destination.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "definition: 'Compelled to view advertisements'",
      "rationale": "The ad cannot be dismissed by any in-game control and the only exit is killing the game, so the player is compelled to view it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection second arm: interface obstructing an opt-out (close/exit control absent) and directing attention toward an option (the store)",
      "rationale": "The absence of any close control plus tap-anywhere-routes-to-store describes UI that blocks exit and funnels interaction to a purchase destination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer states that uninstalling the game would wipe all of their progress and in-game purchases, and expresses reluctance to let that happen ('I really don't want that to happen'), which in turn makes them hesitant to spend more money. The actual request — that the developer add account-bound cloud saving — is a suggestion for a non-existent feature and is not codable under R12. But the review also asserts a currently present game behavior: progress and purchases are not saved to an account and are forfeited if the player leaves. That makes leaving costly because the player would abandon everything they have accumulated, and the reviewer explicitly lets this deter them from deleting the game. This is the invested-value arm of Easy to Get, Hard to Lose.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I know if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value indicator / canonical definition: accumulated investment raises the cost of leaving; exit entails forfeiting value the player already holds.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer says deleting the game forfeits their purchases and hard work and that they don't want that, showing accumulated investment operating on their decision to stay and to hold back further spending — reluctant to abandon gains."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "This is a wholly positive, promotional-style review. It praises graphics, storyline, strategic depth in building an empire, and real-time player-versus-player combat. There is no mention of payment, ads, timers, grinding, rewards, or any monetisation or retention mechanic. The only mechanic the text names that corresponds to a label is PvP combat: 'battling other players in real-time combat' directly describes the game placing players against each other. Phrases like 'kept me hooked from the start' and 'captivating storyline' are evaluative praise, not descriptions of a designed retention mechanism, so they do not lift to Easy to Get Hard to Lose. 'Depth of strategy required to build and expand my empire' is a general description of gameplay depth, not repetitive tasking, so it does not lift to Grinding. The positive tone does not suppress a label once the mechanic is named (R7).",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition: 'Patterns that make players against each other in competition.' Indicator: 'The game makes you compete against other players.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes the game's PvP mechanic as battling other players in real-time, which places players against each other in competition; approval of the feature does not suppress the label under R7."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing energy as a progression gate at high level. At level 113 they report there is no extra energy available, and the only way to keep making progress is to purchase. The complaint is not about waiting for energy to refill (no timer or regeneration schedule is described), nor about a currency exchange. It is a straight statement that continued progression is contingent on payment: a pay wall.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall (Sousa & Oliveira 2023): 'The game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states explicitly that progressing at their current level requires purchasing, matching the pay wall definition directly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes the game's core loop as energy-gated: running out of energy stops all further progress, and the recovery timer is long. That is a wait imposed by the game's own clock that blocks advancement until it resets. The rest of the review consists of design praise and wishes for future features (console release, storyline, customization), which are hypothetical additions and not current mechanics under R12.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play (Hadan et al. 2024): 'Patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers'; darkpattern.games: 'In-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something.' Boundary rule: Wait to Play requires the span to describe the wait or its regeneration schedule (energy refilling over time).",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes an energy meter that refills on a long timer and states that running out blocks further progress, which is exactly the game-imposed schedule the player must wait on."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
