}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a belief that after they began purchasing Poké Balls, capture attempts started failing more often — Pokémon keep breaking out — which makes them consume balls faster and therefore forces them to buy more. This is an allegation that the game secretly manipulates catch outcomes in response to the player's spending, i.e., a hidden schedule governing the determination of outcomes (Illusion of Control's alleged-rigging rule). It also describes continued play (catching) as requiring repeated purchases of balls, i.e., payment needed to keep going (Pay to Progress). The second half of the review disclaims authorship and describes no mechanic. No term needs an external lookup: Poké Balls and capture attempts are self-explanatory in context.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes, a difficulty or schedule hidden from the player.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts the game's capture outcomes were secretly altered once they started paying, engineering failures to drive more purchases — a claimed manipulation of outcome determination, not an unlucky run."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Skip (definition): payment removes an artificial barrier that directly blocks continued play.",
      "rationale": "The span states the player must purchase more balls to keep capturing, meaning continued play is contingent on spending, and the reviewer says this is deliberately engineered."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing two things about progression in Candy Crush Saga. First, hard levels take a week to pass, which is a statement that progress is slow and time-consuming, but no repeated activity is named and no designed wait or timer is described, so it is neither Grinding nor Playing by Appointment. Second, tasks are described as impossible unless the player buys boosters, which states that further progress is contingent on payment: a pay wall. The span carries the payment-for-progress relation directly.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall (Sousa & Oliveira 2023): 'The game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that tasks cannot be passed without purchasing boosters, making continued progress contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a positive recommendation praising the game and, most concretely, its in-app purchase value. The only game mechanic actually described is monetization: the reviewer compares spending real money in other games (where $20 yields less than hoped) with Bingo Blitz, where the same purchase returns twice the quantity. That describes the transaction of real money being converted into an in-game quantity (packs of credits/items) at an exchange rate the reviewer approves of. Everything else — being fun, energizing, having modes and events — is generic praise with no specific mechanic, schedule, competition, or social design named. The 'modes and events' mention is too vague to code as Playing by Appointment or Competition. The only codable mechanic is the real-money-to-in-game-goods conversion.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "If your spend $20 on some item you get less then what your actually hoping for, but on here bingo gives you twice the number of your purchase",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency indicator: real money is the consideration and the conversion into an in-game quantity is described in the span; boundary rule on transaction direction (currency is the object acquired with real money)",
      "rationale": "The span describes spending $20 (real money) and receiving twice the number of the purchased in-game quantity, i.e., the exchange rate between real-world money and in-game currency/goods; the reviewer's approval does not suppress the mechanic (R7)."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event in MONOPOLY GO. They had nearly reached a reward tier (525 dice, 3 tasks left), then ran out of dice. The event counter showed two days remaining, but after the player slept, the event had already rotated and the unclaimed reward was gone. The mechanic being described is an event that operates on the game's clock: the player must complete tasks within the game's schedule or forfeit the reward. The reviewer's frustration is that a partially completed goal was lost because they could not keep playing immediately. There is no bug claim; the event simply changed while they were away. The underlying rule is that event rewards expire with the event window, which imposes the penalty for not playing within the schedule.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play / Evil Battle Pass — patterns that force players to play to the game's schedule; rewards forfeited by not playing within the window",
      "rationale": "The event's countdown set the playing obligation; the player lost a near-complete reward because the game's schedule rolled over while they were away, so progress is lost on the game's clock."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Monopoly GO dice event reward expire",
  "search_result": "Monopoly GO event rewards and dice reward links are time-limited and expire; rewards must be claimed or completed within the event window before the event rotates."
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes several distinct Monopoly GO experiences across one session. First, they complain that friend-invite rewards stopped after the season change: the sentence reveals the game's design of rewarding players for recruiting friends to join via Facebook, and the complaint is that the reward no longer pays out. Second, they report difficulty obtaining dice and a losing run of no Shut downs or bank heists at max bet: this is a bad-luck streak, not an assertion that the game rigs outcomes, so it does not carry Illusion of Control. Third, 'Stickers are duplicates all the way' describes the sticker pack reward system giving repeated duplicate drops from a randomised pool. Finally, the login/network error is a technical fault and is not a deliberate design pattern, so it is not codable.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "Suddenly I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: 'Players are incentivized to invite friends to join the game' / 'You get a bonus for inviting your friends'",
      "rationale": "The sentence reveals the game's recruitment mechanic — inviting friends to join via Facebook is rewarded — and the reviewer complains the reward stopped, which still describes the friend-invite incentive design (R7/R8)."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Reward Mania boundary rule: 'duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool'",
      "rationale": "The span describes the sticker-pack reward delivery as producing repeated duplicates from a randomised pool, which is the variable-reward delivery mechanic sustaining engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a UI behaviour in the game: whenever the app is opened or the 1v1 lobby is entered, the interface automatically scrolls to and pre-selects the highest-stakes bet table affordable with the player's coins. The reviewer reads this as a deliberate \"Dark UX\" trick meant to provoke accidental taps, causing the player to lose accumulated coins and then be pressured into buying more with real money. The complaint is built around the default selection with no opt-out (they ask for a \"Default Table Lock\" and for the game to open on the lowest table by default). The review also establishes that the in-game coins are purchasable with real money (\"forced to buy real ones\"), i.e., an intermediate currency. There is no claim about rigged match outcomes or random reward delivery, so Illusion of Control and Gambling are not supported; the mechanics described are the interface's default-selection misdirection and the real-money purchase of the currency.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection definition and boundary rule: the interface directs the player toward a specific high-stakes option through a default selection, and the span attributes the design to a purpose served against the player's intent (forcing accidental mis-clicks); the requested \"Default Table Lock\" shows there is no opt-out from the auto-selection.",
      "rationale": "The auto-scroll/auto-select is a default UI behaviour that steers the player into an unintended high-stakes action and causes accidental mis-clicks, which is exactly the interface directing attention/selection against the player's intent with no opt-out."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition and boundary rule: where the currency is the object acquired (buy coins) with real money as the consideration, the conversion is in the span.",
      "rationale": "The span shows coins (in-game currency) being purchased with real money, establishing the real-money-to-currency conversion that defines Intermediate Currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two game behaviours. First, an accusation that the randomness is rigged: the RNG is 'so obviously against you,' which asserts the game manipulates outcome determination rather than reporting an ordinary losing run. Second, an economic complaint about progression: the cost to play a level (2200 of some in-game resource) roughly equals or exceeds the reward for winning, so the free path is 'not rewarding' and the alternative is spending actual money. The mention of 'actual money' implies a paid alternative to a deficient free route, tying the economy complaint to progression rather than to a purely cosmetic or convenience purchase. The 'rewards need a big revamp' line is a value complaint and does not describe a reward-delivery mechanism, so Reward Mania does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule, Free alternative: the phrase 'without spending actual money' establishes a paid alternative, and the free path is described as unrewarding, so the player is pushed to pay to progress.",
      "rationale": "Playing without paying is stated to be unrewarding, implying payment is needed to make progress worthwhile."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule, Alleged rigging: the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes (a rigged RNG) rather than describing an unlucky run.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer claims the game's randomness is deliberately set against the player, which is a claim of hidden manipulation of outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a base-builder war game after a few weeks of play. Three things stand out in what they describe. First, they call the game a 'grinder' — naming the repeated task-cycle required to get anywhere, a statement about how progress is produced rather than just an evaluation. Second, they describe events as unwinnable for a general (non-paying) player: winning is contingent on spending, and skill is explicitly said to play no part ('Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win.'). This is the pay-to-win/pay wall structure, with the 30k figure showing the scale of spending that wins an event. Third, 'win an event' implies competitive events in which players are pitted against each other and ranked by spending, though the review's focus is on the pay gate rather than the rivalry itself. The review is largely negative in tone, but R7 and R8 require coding the mechanics described regardless of approval.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Same old same old grinder.",
      "rule_applied": "definition (Performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress)",
      "rationale": "'Grinder' directly names the repetitive task cycle the game requires for progress, meeting the Grinding definition rather than being a mere quality complaint."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event. Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator (darkpattern.games: 'A player can spend real money to purchase something that gives them an advantage in the game') and Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The span states that winning events is impossible for a general player and that outcomes are determined by spending, not skill — payment grants the decisive advantage and gates event success."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition (Patterns that make players against each other in competition)",
      "rationale": "'Win an event' describes competitive events in which players are pitted against each other for placement, with spenders dominating the outcome; the competition mechanic is implied by the event-winner framing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes the core loop of a base-building survival game. Three clear design behaviors are named: a pay-to-win economy, base upgrade timers that grow long (an arbitrary wait imposed by the game), and endless scaling that produces only more grind with no goal to aim at. The UI is described as deliberately confusing and overwhelming with the explicit purpose of pushing purchases, which is an interface designed against the player's intent. Finally, the alliance system punishes non-members: unallied players get attacked and bullied by others, which is player-versus-player predation rather than any described social obligation to keep playing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win low-level indicator of Pay to Progress (Hadan et al. 2024: 'Patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money')",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states outright that the game is arranged so that paying players gain an advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: 'In-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something.'",
      "rationale": "Base upgrade timers impose waits set by the game's clock, and since upgrades gate progression the appointment is required for progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Performing repetitive and tedious tasks' in order to make progress.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly names the required activity as grinding, tied to the endless scaling of the game."
    },
